// The extension HOST target: everything that runs in Node, inside VS Code.
//
// One of the two build gates (`npm run compile` runs this, then
// tsconfig.webview.json). The pair is what enforces the layer split described in
// CLAUDE.md — this config has NO DOM lib, so a host module reaching for
// `window`/`document` is a build error, and so is a pure `view/*.ts` helper that
// stops being DOM-free. That is the mechanism, not a side effect: those helpers
// are shared by both targets and are compiled HERE, which is what keeps them
// importable from the host as well as the webview bundles.
//
// `.tsx` is excluded on purpose — it is this repo's marker for a browser-context
// module (see src/features/shared/view/host.tsx) and belongs to the webview
// config. TypeScript's `*.ts` include glob does not match `.tsx` anyway; the
// exclude is stated so the intent is not left to a glob subtlety.
//
// `types` is explicit rather than "whatever @types/* happens to be installed",
// so a transitively-installed package cannot quietly reintroduce DOM globals
// that `lib` deliberately leaves out. Package-scoped types (@types/js-yaml and
// friends) still resolve normally through imports — `types` only governs the
// global auto-include.
//
// Tests are excluded here and checked by nothing; that asymmetry is known and
// recorded in tsconfig.webview.json. Do not "fix" it by weakening either gate.
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "module": "commonjs",
    "target": "ES2020",
    "lib": ["ES2020"],
    "types": ["node", "vscode"],
    "strict": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "noEmit": true
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", ".vscode-test", "src/**/*.test.ts", "src/**/*.tsx"]
}
